var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-reference","page":"API reference","text":"This page summarizes the public API exported by CodeEdit.jl. For a guided introduction, see Getting started and Editing code.\n\nThe detailed docstrings for exported names are listed at the end of this page.","title":"API reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Handles","page":"API reference","text":"Handles identify parsed blocks and are the primary objects used for searching and editing.\n\nHandle: create a handle to the block containing a file location or method.\neof_handle: create a handle to the end of a file.\nhandles: collect handles for blocks in files.\nreindex: update existing handles after files changed outside CodeEdit.jl.","title":"Handles"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Searching","page":"API reference","text":"search: search handles and files.\n\nSee Searching source for the main source-search workflow and Finding errors from stacktraces for locating source blocks from stack frames.","title":"Searching"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Edits","page":"API reference","text":"Edits are immutable descriptions of changes. Constructing an edit does not modify the filesystem.\n\nAbstractEdit: abstract supertype for edit values.\nReplace: replace a block.\nDelete: delete a block.\nInsertBefore: insert code before a block.\nInsertAfter: insert code after a block.\nCreateFile: create a new file.\nMoveFile: move or rename a file.\nDeleteFile: delete a file.\nCombine: combine edits into one planned edit.\napply!: apply an edit through an explicit version-control specification.\ndisplayed!: mark an edit as displayed.\n\nThe edit1 * edit2 operator is shorthand for Combine(edit1, edit2). Chaining * appends edits to a combined edit.\n\nCombined edits are planned and validated as a unit. See Editing code and Safety and version control for workflow details and failure modes.","title":"Edits"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Version-control","page":"API reference","text":"Version-control specifications determine how an edit is applied.\n\nVersionControl: describe a version-control backend and default apply! keyword arguments.\nGitVersionControl: git-backed version-control specification.\nNoVersionControl: explicitly apply edits without version control.\n\nVersionControl(\"path\") constructs the appropriate version-control specification for a repository. For git repositories, the displayed value is a GitVersionControl.\n\napply!(repo, edit, message) applies an edit, stages affected paths, and creates a git commit with message if repo is a git repository.\n\napply!(repo, edit; default_message=\"...\") uses a default commit message supplied either in the call or in the VersionControl object.\n\napply!(NoVersionControl(require_view=true), edit) applies without git while still requiring a displayed review.\n\nDisplay, printing, and string(edit) store the exact plan that was shown. When require_view=true, apply! replans the edit and rejects it if the current plan differs from the displayed plan.","title":"Version control"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Convenience-functions","page":"API reference","text":"filepath: return the file path for a handle.\nlines: return the line range for a handle.\ndocstring: extract an attached docstring.\nis_valid: test whether a handle or edit is valid.\nstring(handle): return the block text for a handle.\nstring(edit): return the displayed edit plan and mark the edit as displayed.\ndisplay(handle): show a handle header and source block.\ndisplay(edit): show the edit plan and mark the edit as displayed.","title":"Convenience functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Exported-names","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Exported names"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.Handle","page":"API reference","text":"Handle(path, line, pos=1; parse_as=:auto)\nHandle(method)\nHandle(stackframe)\n\nReference to a parsed source/text block.\n\nThe path-based constructor returns a handle to the block containing (line, pos), or to the next block after that location. The method-based constructor returns a handle to a method definition when source information is available.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.Handle"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.eof_handle","page":"API reference","text":"Return the EOF handle for a file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.eof_handle"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.handles","page":"API reference","text":"handles(path; includes=false, parse_as=:auto)\nhandles(paths; includes=false, parse_as=:auto)\nhandles(root, pattern; includes=false, parse_as=:auto)\n\nReturn handles for all parsed blocks in one file, a collection of files, or files under root matching pattern.\n\nIf includes=true, statically resolvable Julia include(...) paths are followed recursively. parse_as may be :auto, :julia, or :text.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.handles"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.reindex","page":"API reference","text":"reindex(path)\nreindex()\n\nReparse cached files and conservatively preserve handles for uniquely matched blocks.\n\nThe path form reindexes one cached file. The zero-argument form reindexes all cached files that still exist.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.reindex"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.search","page":"API reference","text":"search(handle_set, needle::AbstractString)\n\nSearch an existing handle collection for blocks containing needle.\n\n\n\n\n\nsearch(handle_set, needle::Regex)\n\nSearch an existing handle collection for blocks matching needle.\n\n\n\n\n\nsearch(path::AbstractString, needle::AbstractString; parse_as=:auto)\n\nSearch all blocks in a file.\n\n\n\n\n\nsearch(path::AbstractString, needle::Regex; parse_as=:auto)\n\nSearch all blocks in a file.\n\n\n\n\n\nsearch(paths::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}, needle::AbstractString; parse_as=:auto)\n\nSearch all blocks in a collection of files.\n\n\n\n\n\nsearch(paths::AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}, needle::Regex; parse_as=:auto)\n\nSearch all blocks in a collection of files.\n\n\n\n\n\nsearch(root::AbstractString, pattern::AbstractString, needle::AbstractString; includes=false, parse_as=:auto)\n\nSearch files under root matching pattern.\n\n\n\n\n\nsearch(root::AbstractString, pattern::AbstractString, needle::Regex; includes=false, parse_as=:auto)\n\nSearch files under root matching pattern.\n\n\n\n\n\nsearch(repo::VersionControl, needle::AbstractString)\n\nSearch all handles returned by handles(repo).\n\n\n\n\n\nsearch(repo::VersionControl, needle::Regex)\n\nSearch all handles returned by handles(repo).\n\n\n\n\n\nsearch(repo::VersionControl, trace)\n\nSearch all handles returned by handles(repo) for blocks referenced by a stacktrace or backtrace-like object.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.search"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.AbstractEdit","page":"API reference","text":"AbstractEdit\n\nAbstract supertype for all edit values.\n\nEdit objects describe source or filesystem changes that can be displayed, validated, and then applied with apply!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.AbstractEdit"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.Replace","page":"API reference","text":"Replace(handle::Handle, code::AbstractString)\n\nEdit that replaces the source block referred to by handle with code.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.Replace"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.Delete","page":"API reference","text":"Delete(handle::Handle)\n\nEdit that deletes the source block referred to by handle.\n\nDeleting an EOF handle has no effect.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.Delete"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.InsertBefore","page":"API reference","text":"InsertBefore(handle::Handle, code::AbstractString)\n\nEdit that inserts code immediately before the source block referred to by handle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.InsertBefore"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.InsertAfter","page":"API reference","text":"InsertAfter(handle::Handle, code::AbstractString)\n\nEdit that inserts code immediately after the source block referred to by handle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.InsertAfter"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.CreateFile","page":"API reference","text":"CreateFile(path::AbstractString, code::AbstractString; parse_as::Symbol=:auto)\n\nEdit that creates a new file at path containing code.\n\nparse_as may be :auto, :julia, or :text.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.CreateFile"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.MoveFile","page":"API reference","text":"MoveFile(old_path::AbstractString, new_path::AbstractString)\n\nEdit that moves or renames a file from old_path to new_path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.MoveFile"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.DeleteFile","page":"API reference","text":"DeleteFile(path::AbstractString)\n\nEdit that deletes the file at path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.DeleteFile"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.Combine","page":"API reference","text":"Combine(edits::AbstractEdit...)\nCombine(edits::AbstractVector{<:AbstractEdit})\n\nEdit that combines multiple edits into one planned operation.\n\nCombined edits are interpreted in order and validated as a unit. Applying a combined edit that touches multiple files is best-effort at the filesystem level, so a later filesystem failure can leave earlier operations applied.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.Combine"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.apply!","page":"API reference","text":"apply!(vc::VersionControl, edit::AbstractEdit)\n\nApplies an edit using the provided version control schema.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.apply!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.displayed!","page":"API reference","text":"Set or clear the displayed marker for an edit.\n\nWhen displayed is true, this compiles and validates the current edit plan and stores its fingerprint as the plan approved for application. It does not print the diff. Use this only when intentionally bypassing visible review.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.displayed!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.VersionControl","page":"API reference","text":"Version-control specification used by apply!.\n\nvc_type is typically Val(:git) or Val(:none). kwargs stores default keyword arguments for later apply! calls.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.VersionControl"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.GitVersionControl","page":"API reference","text":"A git version control specification.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.GitVersionControl"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CodeEdit.NoVersionControl","page":"API reference","text":"A \"version control\" specification that uses no version control.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.NoVersionControl"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.filepath","page":"API reference","text":"Return the path associated with a handle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.filepath"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.lines","page":"API reference","text":"Return the line range associated with a handle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.lines"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.docstring","page":"API reference","text":"Return a handle's docstring text, if available.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.docstring"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CodeEdit.is_valid","page":"API reference","text":"is_valid(handle)\nis_valid(edit)\n\nReturn whether a handle currently refers to a valid block, or whether an edit can be applied without validation errors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CodeEdit.is_valid"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Blocks-and-handles","page":"Blocks and handles","text":"CodeEdit.jl edits files by first splitting them into source or text blocks. A Handle is a stable reference to one such block and is the object passed to search and edit operations.\n\nEdits built from handles can be applied through git or through an explicit no-version-control specification.","title":"Blocks and handles"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Blocks","page":"Blocks and handles","text":"For Julia files, blocks are top-level syntactic units such as functions, types, macros, constants, assignments, imports, exports, and includes. Attached docstrings stay with the block they document, so replacing a documented function keeps the docstring and function together.\n\nFor example, CodeEdit.jl sees a file like this as several separate blocks:\n\nmodule Inventory              # block\n\nconst DEFAULT_TAX = 0.25       # block\n\nfunction price_with_tax(x)     # block\n    return x * (1 + DEFAULT_TAX)\nend\n\nend                           # block\n                              # EOF block\n\nA Julia module is split into separate blocks. The module ... line and its matching end line are separate blocks, while the module body is subdivided normally.\n\nAt the end of each file, CodeEdit.jl creates a special EOF block. EOF handles are useful when inserting code at the end of a file.\n\nFor non-Julia files, blocks are paragraphs separated by blank lines.","title":"Blocks"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Handles","page":"Blocks and handles","text":"A Handle points to one parsed block. It is the object passed to search, display, and edit operations.\n\njulia> h = Handle(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\", 14)\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl 13 - 15:\nfunction increment(x)\n    return x + 1\nend\n\nBecause line 14 is inside increment, the handle refers to the whole increment block.\n\nHandles are interned for a parsed file: requesting the same block again returns the same handle object.\n\njulia> h === Handle(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\", 13)\ntrue","title":"Handles"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Julia-and-text-parsing","page":"Blocks and handles","text":"By default, .jl files are parsed as Julia source and other files are parsed as text. Use parse_as=:julia or parse_as=:text to override this behavior when constructing or collecting handles.\n\njulia> handles(\"examples/notes.txt\"; parse_as=:text)\n3 handles\n# examples/notes.txt:\n  1 - 1: First note.\n  3 - 3: Second note.\n  EOF:\n\nA cached file has one parse mode at a time. Reloading the same file with a different parse mode invalidates existing handles for that file.","title":"Julia and text parsing"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Handle-validity","page":"Blocks and handles","text":"Edits update handles when their referenced block can still be matched after the edit. Handles are invalidated when their block is deleted or can no longer be matched unambiguously.\n\nUse is_valid to test whether a handle still refers to a valid block.\n\njulia> is_valid(h)\ntrue\n\nFiles modified outside CodeEdit.jl are reparsed automatically when a cached timestamp changes. Call reindex to update cached handles explicitly:\n\njulia> reindex(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\");","title":"Handle validity"},{"category":"section","location":"editing/#Editing-code","page":"Editing code","text":"Editing in CodeEdit.jl separates description from execution: first construct an edit value, then choose how to apply it.\n\nHandle -> Edit -> Displayed plan -> Apply -> Commit\n\nEdits are values that subtype AbstractEdit. Constructing an edit describes an intended change to one or more handles or paths.\n\nThe standard workflow uses VersionControl to apply the edit, stage the affected paths, and create a git commit. If require_view=true, displaying, printing, or stringifying an edit records the exact plan that was shown. apply! replans the edit and refuses to apply it if the current plan differs from the displayed plan.\n\nIn doctest examples, omitting the semicolon from the edit = ... line displays the edit and marks it as displayed. Calling display(edit) has the same effect.\n\njulia> repo = VersionControl(\"examples\"; require_view=true)\nGitVersionControl(\"examples\"; require_view=true)\n","title":"Editing code"},{"category":"section","location":"editing/#Choosing-an-edit-operation","page":"Editing code","text":"Most edits correspond to one of the following operations:\n\nreplace an existing block with Replace;\ninsert code near an existing block with InsertBefore or InsertAfter;\nappend code with eof_handle and InsertBefore: the EOF handle is a zero-width anchor at the end of the file;\ndelete a block with Delete;\ncreate, move, or delete whole files;\ngroup related edits with Combine.\n\nThe sections below follow that progression.","title":"Choosing an edit operation"},{"category":"section","location":"editing/#Replacing-a-block","page":"Editing code","text":"A replacement edit changes exactly the block referenced by a handle. This is usually the safest way to update a function, because the planned diff is limited to the selected block.\n\njulia> h = Handle(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\", 14)\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl 13 - 15:\nfunction increment(x)\n    return x + 1\nend\n\njulia> new_code = replace(string(h), \"x + 1\" => \"x + 2\");\n\njulia> edit = Replace(h, new_code)\nEdit modifies examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n14c14\n<     return x + 1\n---\n>     return x + 2\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Change increment\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit 67decaf\n","title":"Replacing a block"},{"category":"section","location":"editing/#Inserting-code","page":"Editing code","text":"Insertion edits are useful when a nearby block provides a stable anchor point.\n\nInsert before a block:\n\njulia> h = Handle(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\", 14)\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl 13 - 15:\nfunction increment(x)\n    return x + 2\nend\n\njulia> edit = InsertBefore(h, raw\"\"\"\n       const SCALE = 2\n       \n       \"\"\")\nEdit modifies examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n12c13,14\n---\n> const SCALE = 2\n>\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Add scale constant\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit 8c0ffee\n\n\nInsert after a block:\n\njulia> h = Handle(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\", 16)\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl 15 - 17:\nfunction increment(x)\n    return x + 2\nend\n\njulia> edit = InsertAfter(h, raw\"\"\"\n       \n       function scaled_increment(x)\n           return increment(x) * SCALE\n       end\n       \"\"\")\nEdit modifies examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n18c19,22\n---\n> function scaled_increment(x)\n>     return increment(x) * SCALE\n> end\n>\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Add scaled_increment\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit c0ffeed\n\n\nUse raw string literals such as raw\"\"\"...\"\"\" when writing Julia code as strings. They avoid accidental escaping of backslashes and dollar signs.\n\nInserted text is used exactly as provided. Include leading or trailing newlines when you want blank lines around the inserted code.","title":"Inserting code"},{"category":"section","location":"editing/#Deleting-code","page":"Editing code","text":"julia> h = only(search(handles(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\"), \"function obsolete\"))\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl 27 - 29:\nfunction obsolete()\n    return :remove_me\nend\n\njulia> edit = Delete(h)\nEdit modifies examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n27,29c26\n< function obsolete()\n<     return :remove_me\n< end\n---\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Remove obsolete function\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit 123cafe\n\n\nDeleting an EOF handle has no effect and is usually not useful.","title":"Deleting code"},{"category":"section","location":"editing/#Creating,-moving,-and-deleting-files","page":"Editing code","text":"julia> edit = CreateFile(\"examples/generated.jl\", raw\"\"\"\n       function generated_value()\n           return :ok\n       end\n       \"\"\")\nEdit creates examples/generated.jl:\n0c1,3\n---\n> function generated_value()\n>     return :ok\n> end\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Add generated file\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit 1bada55\n\n\njulia> edit = MoveFile(\"examples/generated.jl\", \"examples/generated-renamed.jl\")\nEdit moves examples/generated.jl -> examples/generated-renamed.jl\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Rename generated file\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit 2facade\n\n\njulia> edit = DeleteFile(\"examples/generated-renamed.jl\")\nEdit deletes examples/generated-renamed.jl\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Remove generated file\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit deada55\n","title":"Creating, moving, and deleting files"},{"category":"section","location":"editing/#Combining-edits","page":"Editing code","text":"Use Combine, or the * shorthand, when multiple edits are part of one logical change and should be planned together:\n\njulia> source = only(search(handles(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\"), \"function old_function_name\"))\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl 23 - 25:\nfunction old_function_name()\n    return foo(1)\nend\n\njulia> destination = eof_handle(\"examples/notes.txt\")\n# examples/notes.txt EOF:\n\njulia> edit = Combine(\n           InsertBefore(destination, \"\\nMoved selected source:\\n\\n\" * string(source)),\n           Delete(source),\n       )\nEdit modifies examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n23,25c22\n< function old_function_name()\n<     return foo(1)\n< end\n---\nEdit modifies examples/notes.txt:\n3c4,9\n---\n>\n> Moved selected source:\n>\n> function old_function_name()\n>     return foo(1)\n> end\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Move selected source to notes\")\nApplied: 2 files changed, commit 12feed3\n\n\nEquivalent shorthand:\n\njulia> h = only(search(handles(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\"), \"function increment\"))\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl 15 - 17:\nfunction increment(x)\n    return x + 2\nend\n\njulia> edit = InsertAfter(h, raw\"\"\"\n       \n       function bounded_increment(x)\n           return min(increment(x), DEFAULT_LIMIT)\n       end\n       \"\"\") * InsertBefore(eof_handle(\"examples/notes.txt\"), \"\\nAdded bounded_increment to DemoPackage.jl\\n\")\nEdit modifies examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n18c19,22\n---\n> function bounded_increment(x)\n>     return min(increment(x), DEFAULT_LIMIT)\n> end\n>\nEdit modifies examples/notes.txt:\n9c10,11\n---\n>\n> Added bounded_increment to DemoPackage.jl\n\n\nCombined edits are validated after the full combined result is planned. Intermediate states may therefore be invalid Julia syntax, provided the final result is valid.\n\nPlanning and validation are all-or-nothing. Applying a combined edit that touches multiple files is still best-effort at the filesystem level: if a later filesystem operation fails, earlier operations may already have been applied. Use version control so changes can be reviewed and recovered.","title":"Combining edits"},{"category":"section","location":"editing/#Applying-edits-without-version-control","page":"Editing code","text":"For scratch files, generated files, or other changes that should not create a commit, pass an explicit NoVersionControl specification.\n\nNoVersionControl makes uncommitted edits explicit at the call site.\n\njulia> write(\"scratch-note.txt\", \"status = old\\n\")\n13\n\njulia> h = Handle(\"scratch-note.txt\", 1; parse_as=:text)\n# scratch-note.txt 1 - 1:\nstatus = old\n\njulia> edit = Replace(h, \"status = new\\n\")\nEdit modifies scratch-note.txt:\n1c1\n< status = old\n---\n> status = new\n\njulia> apply!(NoVersionControl(require_view=true), edit)\nApplied: 1 file changed\n","title":"Applying edits without version control"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Getting-started","page":"Getting started","text":"This chapter introduces the basic CodeEdit.jl workflow: find a block, construct an edit, review the plan, and apply it deliberately.\n\nThe documentation examples share a small git repository in examples and commit each successful source edit.","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Installation","page":"Getting started","text":"Install CodeEdit.jl with Julia's package manager. If the package is not registered, add it from its repository URL:\n\npkg> add https://github.com/perrutquist/CodeEdit.jl","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Loading-the-package","page":"Getting started","text":"julia> using CodeEdit","title":"Loading the package"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Creating-a-handle","page":"Getting started","text":"CodeEdit.jl starts from source locations and edits blocks rather than raw line ranges. Use Handle to refer to the block containing a location:\n\njulia> h = Handle(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\", 10)\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl 7 - 11:\nfunction foo(x)\n    y = helper(x)\n    z = y * 2\n    return z\nend\n\n\nIf line 10 is inside a function, h refers to the whole function block.\n\njulia> source = string(h)\n\"function foo(x)\\n    y = helper(x)\\n    z = y * 2\\n    return z\\nend\\n\"","title":"Creating a handle"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Listing-and-searching-handles","page":"Getting started","text":"List the parsed blocks in a file with handles:\n\njulia> hs = handles(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\")\n9 handles\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n   1 -  1: module DemoPackage\n   3 -  3: include(\"helpers.jl\")\n   5 -  5: const DEFAULT_LIMIT = 10\n   7 - 11: function foo(x); y = helper(x); z = y * …\n  13 - 15: function increment(x); return x + 1; end\n  17 - 19: function old_function_name(); return foo…\n  21 - 23: function obsolete(); return :remove_me; …\n  25 - 25: end\n  EOF:\n\nSearch within those handles to find a block by text:\n\njulia> matches = search(hs, \"old_function_name\")\n1 handle\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n  17 - 19: function old_function_name(); return foo…\n\nThe result can be inspected, displayed, or used as the target of an edit. See Searching source for glob searches, regex searches, and recursive include traversal.","title":"Listing and searching handles"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Applying-an-edit-with-git","page":"Getting started","text":"Inspecting handles leaves files unchanged. To change source, construct an edit value and choose how it should be applied.\n\nFor ordinary source changes, use a git-backed version-control specification:\n\njulia> repo = VersionControl(\"examples\"; require_view=true)\nGitVersionControl(\"examples\"; require_view=true)\n\nWith require_view=true, displaying the edit records the exact plan. When apply! runs, CodeEdit.jl plans the edit again and refuses to apply it if the current plan differs from the displayed one:\n\nnote: Note\nIn the REPL, evaluating an edit without a trailing semicolon displays it. Calling display(edit) is equivalent.\n\njulia> h = only(search(hs, \"old_function_name\"));\n\njulia> edit = Replace(h, replace(string(h), \"old_function_name\" => \"new_function_name\"))\nEdit modifies examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n17c17\n< function old_function_name()\n---\n> function new_function_name()\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Rename old_function_name\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit 3630f3e\n\nThe edit is written to disk and committed to git. Each successful git-backed edit is committed with the message you provide.","title":"Applying an edit with git"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Inserting-at-the-end-of-a-file","page":"Getting started","text":"Use eof_handle when inserting new code at the end of a file:\n\njulia> h = eof_handle(\"examples/helpers.jl\");\n\njulia> edit = InsertBefore(h, raw\"\"\"\n       \n       another_helper(x) = helper(x) * 2\n       \"\"\")\nEdit modifies examples/helpers.jl:\n1c2,3\n---\n>\n> another_helper(x) = helper(x) * 2\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Add another helper\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit c58b1c4","title":"Inserting at the end of a file"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Applying-without-version-control","page":"Getting started","text":"For generated files, scratch files, or other changes that should not create a commit, pass an explicit NoVersionControl specification:\n\njulia> write(\"scratch.txt\", \"temporary = false\\n\");\n\njulia> h = Handle(\"scratch.txt\", 1; parse_as=:text);\n\njulia> edit = Replace(h, \"temporary = true\\n\")\nEdit modifies scratch.txt:\n1c1\n< temporary = false\n---\n> temporary = true\n\njulia> apply!(NoVersionControl(require_view=true), edit)\nApplied: 1 file changed","title":"Applying without version control"},{"category":"section","location":"searching-errors/#Finding-errors-from-stacktraces","page":"Finding errors from a stacktrace","text":"CodeEdit.jl can locate source blocks referenced by a stacktrace. This makes a debugging session into an editing workflow: catch the error, capture the stacktrace with catch_backtrace(), map frames to handles, inspect the matching blocks, then edit the source and commit the fix.","title":"Finding errors from stacktraces"},{"category":"section","location":"searching-errors/#Starting-from-a-stacktrace","page":"Finding errors from a stacktrace","text":"Suppose the following call throws an exception:\n\nfunction inner(x)\n    error(\"bad input: $x\")\nend\n\nfunction outer(x)\n    return inner(x + 1)\nend\n\nThe thrown error contains stack frames for both functions:\n\njulia> outer(1)\nERROR: bad input: 2\nStacktrace:\n [1] error(s::String)\n   @ Base ./error.jl:44\n [2] inner(x::Int64)\n   @ Main .../docs/examples/error-example.jl:2\n [3] outer(x::Int64)\n   @ Main .../docs/examples/error-example.jl:6\n\nCapture the backtrace in a variable:\n\njulia> trace = try\n           outer(1)\n       catch\n           stacktrace(catch_backtrace())\n       end;","title":"Starting from a stacktrace"},{"category":"section","location":"searching-errors/#Inspecting-the-most-relevant-blocks","page":"Finding errors from a stacktrace","text":"Let's display the intersection of (blocks in our code) with (blocks in the trace), in the order that they appear in the trace.\n\njulia> hs = handles(\"examples\", \"*.jl\");\n\njulia> for h in Handle.(trace)\n          if h in hs\n              println(h)\n          end\n       end\n# examples/error-example.jl 1 - 3:\nfunction inner(x)\n    error(\"bad input: $x\")\nend\n\n# examples/error-example.jl 5 - 7:\nfunction outer(x)\n    return inner(x + 1)\nend\n\n\nA displayed handle includes the file name and line range, followed by the source block.","title":"Inspecting the most relevant blocks"},{"category":"section","location":"searching-errors/#Editing-after-locating-the-error","page":"Finding errors from a stacktrace","text":"After finding the relevant block, construct a replacement and apply it through git:\n\njulia> repo = VersionControl(\"examples\"; require_view=true);\n\njulia> h = only(search(intersect(hs, Handle.(trace)), \"error(\"))\n# examples/error-example.jl 1 - 3:\nfunction inner(x)\n    error(\"bad input: $x\")\nend\n\njulia> fixed = replace(string(h), \"error(\\\"bad input: \\$x\\\")\" => \"throw(ArgumentError(\\\"bad input: \\$x\\\"))\");\n\njulia> edit = Replace(h, fixed)\nEdit modifies examples/error-example.jl:\n2c2\n<     error(\"bad input: $x\")\n---\n>     throw(ArgumentError(\"bad input: $x\"))\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Throw ArgumentError for bad input\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit 751de5e\n\nAfter a successful edit, existing handles are updated or invalidated as needed.\n\nFor ordinary string, regex, glob, and recursive include searches, see Searching source.","title":"Editing after locating the error"},{"category":"section","location":"searching/#Searching-source","page":"Searching source","text":"CodeEdit.jl searches parsed blocks rather than raw line ranges. Search results are handles, so any result can be displayed, inspected, or used as the target of an edit.\n\nThe usual pattern is:\n\nCollect handles -> Search handles -> Inspect matches -> Edit a match","title":"Searching source"},{"category":"section","location":"searching/#Searching-a-file","page":"Searching source","text":"Collect handles for one file with handles, then search them by string:\n\njulia> hs = handles(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\");\n\njulia> search(hs, \"old_function_name\")\n1 handle\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n  17 - 19: function old_function_name(); return foo…\n\nDisplay a match to see the full block:\n\njulia> h = only(search(hs, \"old_function_name\"));\n\njulia> println(h)\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl 17 - 19:\nfunction old_function_name()\n    return foo(1)\nend\n","title":"Searching a file"},{"category":"section","location":"searching/#Regex-searches","page":"Searching source","text":"Use a regular expression when the exact text is not known:\n\njulia> search(hs, r\"function .*increment\")\n1 handle\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n  13 - 15: function increment(x); return x + 1; end","title":"Regex searches"},{"category":"section","location":"searching/#Searching-files-and-directories","page":"Searching source","text":"You can collect handles from files matching a glob:\n\njulia> hs = handles(\"examples\", \"*.jl\")\n14 handles\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n   1 -  1: module DemoPackage\n   3 -  3: include(\"helpers.jl\")\n   5 -  5: const DEFAULT_LIMIT = 10\n   7 - 11: function foo(x); y = helper(x); z = y * …\n  13 - 15: function increment(x); return x + 1; end\n  17 - 19: function old_function_name(); return foo…\n  21 - 23: function obsolete(); return :remove_me; …\n  25 - 25: end\n  EOF:\n\n# examples/error-example.jl:\n  1 - 3: function inner(x); error(\"bad input: $x\"…\n  5 - 7: function outer(x); return inner(x + 1); …\n  EOF:\n\n# examples/helpers.jl:\n  1 - 1: helper(x) = x + 1\n  EOF:\n\nFor common cases, search can collect handles and search in one call:\n\njulia> search(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\", \"DEFAULT_LIMIT\")\n1 handle\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n  5 - 5: const DEFAULT_LIMIT = 10","title":"Searching files and directories"},{"category":"section","location":"searching/#Following-includes","page":"Searching source","text":"For Julia entry-point files, pass includes=true to follow include statements recursively:\n\njulia> handles(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\"; includes = true)\n11 handles\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n   1 -  1: module DemoPackage\n   3 -  3: include(\"helpers.jl\")\n   5 -  5: const DEFAULT_LIMIT = 10\n   7 - 11: function foo(x); y = helper(x); z = y * …\n  13 - 15: function increment(x); return x + 1; end\n  17 - 19: function old_function_name(); return foo…\n  21 - 23: function obsolete(); return :remove_me; …\n  25 - 25: end\n  EOF:\n\n# examples/helpers.jl:\n  1 - 1: helper(x) = x + 1\n  EOF:\n\nRecursive include traversal uses cycle detection, so include loops are visited at most once.","title":"Following includes"},{"category":"section","location":"searching/#Result-order","page":"Searching source","text":"Search results are handle sets. Their displayed summary is grouped by file, but iteration over the set will yield an arbitrary order. If order matters, sort results explicitly using filepath and lines. The command sort!(collect(hs), by=CodeEdit.handle_sort_key) will generate a sorted Vector from the set hs in the exact order that it is displayed.","title":"Result order"},{"category":"section","location":"searching/#Extracting-a-single-handle-from-a-search","page":"Searching source","text":"A search or filter operation that returns a single handle can be passed to the only function to extract that handle, for example:\n\njulia> h1 = only(search(hs, \"function foo\"))\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl 7 - 11:\nfunction foo(x)\n    y = helper(x)\n    z = y * 2\n    return z\nend\n\n\nA simple way is often to use the Handle constructor with the filename and line number, although this can be brittle as line numbers can change due to edits.\n\njulia> h2 = Handle(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\", 10);\n\njulia> h3 = only(filter(h -> occursin(\"DemoPackage\", filepath(h)) && 10 in lines(h), hs));\n\njulia> h1 === h2 === h3\ntrue\n","title":"Extracting a single handle from a search"},{"category":"section","location":"#CodeEdit.jl","page":"Home","text":"CodeEdit.jl provides tools for making source edits from Julia. Edits are described as ordinary Julia values, displayed as diffs, and applied explicitly.\n\nCodeEdit.jl operates on parsed source blocks. A Handle identifies a block; an edit such as Replace or InsertAfter describes a change to that block; apply! writes the result through a version-control backend or through NoVersionControl.","title":"CodeEdit.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Manual","page":"Home","text":"Getting started\nBlocks and handles\nSearching source\nEditing code\nSafety and version control\nFinding errors from stacktraces\nAPI reference","title":"Manual"},{"category":"section","location":"#Source-model","page":"Home","text":"CodeEdit.jl represents source locations with Handles. Each handle refers to one parsed block in a file. For Julia files, blocks are top-level syntactic units such as functions, types, constants, imports, exports, and includes. For text files, blocks are paragraphs separated by blank lines.\n\nSee Blocks and handles for the exact rules.","title":"Source model"},{"category":"section","location":"#Basic-workflow","page":"Home","text":"The usual workflow is explicit at each step:\n\nHandle -> Edit -> Displayed plan -> Apply -> Commit\n\nThe following example uses the shared documentation repository, changes one function, applies the edit, and reads the edited block back from disk.\n\njulia> repo = VersionControl(\"examples\"; require_view=true)\nGitVersionControl(\"examples\"; require_view=true)\n\njulia> h = Handle(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\", 14)\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl 13 - 15:\nfunction increment(x)\n    return x + 1\nend\n\njulia> replacement = replace(string(h), \"x + 1\" => \"x + 2\");\n\njulia> edit = Replace(h, replacement)\nEdit modifies examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n14c14\n<     return x + 1\n---\n>     return x + 2\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Change increment\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit 4c0ffee\n\njulia> println(string(Handle(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\", 14)));\nfunction increment(x)\n    return x + 2\nend\n\nIf Revise.jl is loaded, CodeEdit.jl calls Revise after a successful edit, so changed method definitions usually take effect immediately.","title":"Basic workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"#Safety-model","page":"Home","text":"Constructing an edit only describes a change. Displaying an edit shows the planned change. With require_view=true, apply! verifies that the displayed plan is still current before writing any files.\n\nThe standard workflow applies edits through git and records each successful edit as a commit. See Safety and version control for details.","title":"Safety model"},{"category":"section","location":"safety/#Safety-and-version-control","page":"Safety and version control","text":"CodeEdit.jl separates planning from application. Constructing an edit value describes a change; applying an edit writes it to the filesystem.\n\nDisplaying an edit shows the planned change. Applying the edit replans it, checks that the result is still valid, writes files, and, in the standard workflow, records the change as a git commit.","title":"Safety and version control"},{"category":"section","location":"safety/#Planning-before-applying","page":"Safety and version control","text":"An edit such as Replace, InsertBefore, or Delete describes an intended change. It can be inspected before it is applied:\n\njulia> handle = Handle(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\", 5)\n# examples/DemoPackage.jl 5 - 5:\nconst DEFAULT_LIMIT = 10\n\njulia> new_source = replace(string(handle), \"10\" => \"20\")\n\"const DEFAULT_LIMIT = 20\\n\"\n\njulia> edit = Replace(handle, new_source)\nEdit modifies examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n5c5\n< const DEFAULT_LIMIT = 10\n---\n> const DEFAULT_LIMIT = 20\n\nWhen require_view=true, CodeEdit.jl stores the exact plan that was displayed. Later, apply! plans the edit again and refuses to apply it if the current plan differs from the displayed plan.\n\nThis protects against applying a stale edit after the surrounding file has changed.\n\nFor example, this edit is displayed against the original file, but the file is changed before apply! runs:\n\njulia> write(\"scratch.txt\", \"status = old\\n\");\n\njulia> handle = Handle(\"scratch.txt\", 1; parse_as=:text);\n\njulia> edit = Replace(handle, \"status = new\\n\")\nEdit modifies scratch.txt:\n1c1\n< status = old\n---\n> status = new\n\njulia> write(\"scratch.txt\", \"status = changed elsewhere\\n\");\n\njulia> apply!(NoVersionControl(require_view=true), edit)\nERROR: displayed edit was invalid\n\nDisplay the edit again to review the current plan before applying it.","title":"Planning before applying"},{"category":"section","location":"safety/#Git-backed-editing","page":"Safety and version control","text":"The standard workflow uses VersionControl:\n\njulia> repo = VersionControl(\"examples\"; require_view=true);\n\njulia> handle = Handle(\"examples/DemoPackage.jl\", 5);\n\njulia> edit = Replace(handle, replace(string(handle), \"10\" => \"20\"))\nEdit modifies examples/DemoPackage.jl:\n5c5\n< const DEFAULT_LIMIT = 10\n---\n> const DEFAULT_LIMIT = 20\n\njulia> apply!(repo, edit, \"Update default limit\")\nApplied: 1 file changed, commit 0000000\n\nA git-backed apply writes the edited files, stages the affected paths, and creates a commit. By default, CodeEdit.jl expects edited files to be tracked by git and rejects creation outside the worktree.\n\nGit is the recommended undo and recovery mechanism for CodeEdit.jl edits.","title":"Git-backed editing"},{"category":"section","location":"safety/#Dirty-files","page":"Safety and version control","text":"CodeEdit.jl can reject edits when relevant tracked files are dirty. This prevents an edit from accidentally mixing with uncommitted changes in the same files.\n\nIf you deliberately want to checkpoint existing dirty work first, supply precommit_message. CodeEdit.jl can then commit the existing changes before formatting or applying the new edit. This supports two workflows:\n\nkeep the relevant files clean before each edit;\nexplicitly checkpoint dirty work before CodeEdit.jl changes anything.\n\nThe important rule is to make the state of the worktree intentional before applying an edit. Do not rely on CodeEdit.jl as an undo stack; use git history for review and recovery.","title":"Dirty files"},{"category":"section","location":"safety/#Applying-without-version-control","page":"Safety and version control","text":"For scratch files, generated files, or temporary changes, use NoVersionControl:\n\njulia> write(\"scratch.txt\", \"temporary = false\\n\");\n\njulia> handle = Handle(\"scratch.txt\", 1; parse_as=:text);\n\njulia> edit = Replace(handle, \"temporary = true\\n\")\nEdit modifies scratch.txt:\n1c1\n< temporary = false\n---\n> temporary = true\n\njulia> apply!(NoVersionControl(require_view=true), edit)\nApplied: 1 file changed\n\nThis mode is explicit by design: the call site states that the edit will not be recorded as a git commit.","title":"Applying without version control"},{"category":"section","location":"safety/#Validation","page":"Safety and version control","text":"Julia files are reparsed before edits are applied. If the final result would introduce syntax errors, the edit is rejected before the invalid source is written:\n\njulia> write(\"scratch.jl\", \"function ok()\\n    return 1\\nend\\n\");\n\njulia> handle = Handle(\"scratch.jl\", 1);\n\njulia> edit = Replace(handle, \"function broken(\\n\")\nEdit modifies scratch.jl:\n1,3c1\n< function ok()\n<     return 1\n< end\n---\n> function broken(\nValidation errors:\n- ArgumentError: Julia file could not be parsed: docs/scratch.jl\n\njulia> apply!(NoVersionControl(require_view=true), edit)\nERROR: displayed edit was invalid\n\nCombined edits are planned and validated as a unit, so intermediate states may be invalid as long as the final result is valid.","title":"Validation"},{"category":"section","location":"safety/#Combined-edits-and-filesystem-failures","page":"Safety and version control","text":"Combine lets several edits be planned together. Planning and validation are all-or-nothing.\n\nApplying a combined edit that touches multiple files is still best-effort at the filesystem level. If an early file operation succeeds and a later one fails, the filesystem can be left partially changed.\n\nUse git-backed editing for source changes you want to review, commit, or recover.","title":"Combined edits and filesystem failures"},{"category":"section","location":"safety/#Limitations","page":"Safety and version control","text":"CodeEdit.jl rejects edits when it cannot replan or validate them safely. Use version control for history, review, and recovery.\n\nIn particular:\n\nmulti-file applies are not atomic at the filesystem level;\nno built-in undo stack is provided;\nhandles can become invalid when their referenced blocks are deleted or can no longer be matched;\nno-version-control edits are not committed unless you commit them yourself.\n\nThe safest workflow is to work in a git repository, require review for edits that matter, and keep each change small.","title":"Limitations"}]
}
